            <div xml:id="d54">

                <pb facs="0076" n="67" sameAs="frus:frus1940v02/pg67"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00112 Wood Pulp/28: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kennedy</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-07-01T00:00:00+01:00">July 1,
                            1940—midnight</date>.<lb/>[Received July 1—8:08 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1920. Your 1042, May 30 [<hi rendition="#italic">31</hi>], 4 p.m. Letter received
                    today from Foreign Office refers to Lord Halifax’s statement that there would be
                    no difficulty from British point of view in passage of Swedish vessels engaged
                    in Swedish-American trade and in particular to outward voyage of four vessels
                    loading wood pulp for the United States (my 1494, June 3, 11 p.m.). Foreign
                    Office states that German Government, however, has definitely refused to allow
                    Swedish ships to trade outside the Baltic and in view of this attitude there is
                    nothing the British can do to help.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kennedy</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
